                    round_name_r1:          `Objectives`,
                    round_name_r2:          `Module Mix`,
                    round_name_r3:          `Assessment`,
                    round_name_r4:          `Pitch & Defend`,

                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Translate the brief into learning objectives`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `Pohjola’s brief says “build the leadership and commercial capability to drive the energy transition.” That is a destination, not a design. The CHRO wants leadership <strong>behaviours</strong>; the commercial COO wants hard <strong>P&L and market-design</strong> capability. Convert the vague aspiration into <strong>4–6 prioritized, measurable learning objectives</strong> — stated as observable manager capabilities — and explicitly decide what the program will <em>not</em> cover. Backward design starts here: get a learning-objective contract both sponsors sign before you design a single module. Try to do everything in 12 days and you satisfy no one.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Design the module mix inside the window`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Allocate the <strong>12 contact days</strong> and the <strong>€669K</strong> deliverable budget across the module mix — core leadership, commercial/energy-market, the €70K custom simulation, peer action-learning, and a capstone transition project. Every module must trace back to a Round 1 objective; cut any orphan. Decide build-vs-buy for the simulation, and whether 1:1 coaching (€1,800/participant) goes to everyone or just where it earns its cost. The trap is your modular instinct to <em>add</em>: stack coaching on all 60 plus two simulations and you blow the per-learner economics before counting faculty.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Choose the assessment & transfer format`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `Design how the program is <strong>assessed</strong> so it demonstrably produces <strong>transfer</strong>, not just attendance — the hardest choice for senior managers who resist “being graded.” A live capstone judged by Pohjola executives proves applied transfer; <strong>360° behavioural evidence</strong> (€240/participant) gives the CHRO behavioural proof. A portfolio is cheap but weak proof to the COO; a written exam is rigorous but kills engagement. Satisfy <em>both</em> sponsors, prove transfer to a live transition project, and protect the <strong>90% completion floor</strong> — without an assessment burden so heavy that busy executives drop out.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Pitch to the steering group & defend the cuts`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Present the full design to Pohjola’s steering group — objectives, traced module mix, assessment, economics. Then the curveball: the commercial COO demands <strong>two additional market-design modules</strong> “without changing the price or the dates.” The fixed 12 days and budget mean something has to give. The disciplined answer puts an <em>explicit trade-off</em> on the table — what comes out, or what the additions cost in days and euros. Saying yes to everything makes the program impossible; a flat refusal loses the client. Report the KPIs and close on a coherent, deliverable, economically sound design.`,

                    r1_approach_label:      `How do you respond to a vague brief and a fixed €880K / 12-day envelope?`,
                    r1_reconcile_label:     `The CHRO wants leadership behaviours; the COO wants hard P&L. How do you reconcile them?`,
                    r1_scope_label:         `How disciplined is your explicit out-of-scope list — what the program will NOT do? (%)`,
                    r1_scope_readout:       `Scoping discipline — willingness to say no:`,
                    approach_contract:      `Contract 4–6 prioritized, measurable objectives first — and an explicit out-of-scope`,
                    approach_everything:    `Design to the brief — build leadership AND commercial AND change capability in 12 days`,
                    approach_topics:        `Start from interesting topics faculty can teach, then fit objectives around them`,
                    approach_sponsor:       `Let the loudest sponsor decide — give the COO the program they describe`,
                    reconcile_ranked:       `One ranked objective set both sign — leadership behaviours applied to commercial transition decisions`,
                    reconcile_chro:         `Tilt to the CHRO — it is a leadership program; the commercial content is secondary`,
                    reconcile_coo:          `Tilt to the COO — it is a commercial program; behaviours are soft and secondary`,
                    reconcile_both:         `Promise both fully — keep everyone happy and sort out the time later`,

                    r2_alloc_label:         `Allocate the 12 contact days across the module mix (must total 12.0 days)`,
                    r2_days_readout:        `Contact days allocated:`,
                    r2_days_cap:            `/ 12.0 days`,
                    r2_coaching_label:      `One-to-one executive coaching (€1,800 / participant) — at what scale?`,
                    r2_buildbuy_label:      `The custom simulation: build, buy, or skip it?`,
                    mod_leadership:         `Core leadership modules — behaviours for the transition`,
                    mod_commercial:         `Commercial / energy-market modules — P&L and market design`,
                    mod_simulation:         `Custom business simulation (€70K · 2 days)`,
                    mod_capstone:           `Capstone transition project — applied to a live Pohjola project`,
                    mod_peer:               `Peer action-learning sets`,
                    coach_targeted:         `Targeted — coaching for the ~20 with the biggest transition role (€36K)`,
                    coach_all:              `All 60 managers get 1:1 coaching (€108K)`,
                    coach_none:             `No 1:1 coaching — rely on peer action-learning instead (€0)`,
                    bb_buy:                 `Commission the €70K custom simulation — it anchors the commercial objective`,
                    bb_skip:                `Skip it — cover market design with a cheaper case study (€8K)`,
                    bb_gold:                `Build two custom simulations to impress the COO (€130K)`,

                    r3_assess_label:        `Choose the assessment format that proves transfer to BOTH sponsors`,
                    r3_360_label:           `360° behavioural assessment (€240 / participant) — include it for the CHRO’s evidence?`,
                    r3_engage_label:        `Design for the 90% completion floor — how much do you engineer engagement vs pure rigour? (%)`,
                    r3_engage_readout:      `Engagement design (applied, manager-involving, not lecture-heavy):`,
                    r3_quality_label:       `Assessment-coordination investment — facilitation of the live capstone (€k)`,
                    r3_quality_hint:        `€k to coordinate executive judges, project sponsors and evidence collection — credible transfer is coordination-heavy.`,
                    assess_blend:           `Live capstone judged by Pohjola executives + 360° behavioural evidence (applied + behavioural)`,
                    assess_portfolio:       `Individual reflective portfolios only — low cost, weak proof to the COO`,
                    assess_simscore:        `Simulation-performance score only — objective but narrow`,
                    assess_exam:            `A graded written exam at the end — rigorous and cheap`,
                    ts_yes:                 `Yes — run the 360 across all 60 (€14.4K), the CHRO’s behavioural proof`,
                    ts_no:                  `No — skip the 360, rely on the capstone alone (€0)`,

                    r4_coo_label:           `The COO demands two more market-design modules “without changing the price or the dates”. You:`,
                    r4_pitch_label:         `Your 3-minute pitch to the steering group leads with:`,
                    r4_kpi_label:           `Which KPIs do you commit to report to Pohjola at the end?`,
                    r4_conviction_label:    `Conviction: how firmly do you defend that disciplined scope beats “yes to everything”? (%)`,
                    coo_tradeoff:           `Put an explicit trade-off on the table — what comes out, or what the additions cost in days and euros`,
                    coo_cave:               `Agree — add both modules at the same price and dates to keep the client happy`,
                    coo_refuse:             `Flatly refuse — “the design is locked”, no alternative offered`,
                    pitch_design:           `The objectives, the traced module mix, the transfer evidence, and the economics — as one coherent case`,
                    pitch_richness:         `The richness — every topic, module and high-touch element you packed in`,
                    pitch_price:            `The price — how much program they got for €880K`,
                    kpi_full:               `Objective fit, completion rate, contribution margin AND cost per learner — the full economics`,
                    kpi_completion:         `Completion rate only — the contractual floor is all that matters`,
                    kpi_none:               `No hard KPIs — present testimonials and satisfaction scores instead`,

                    warn_scope_range:       `Scoping discipline must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_days_negative:     `Contact-day allocations cannot be negative.`,
                    warn_days_total:        `The module mix must total exactly 12.0 contact days — the cap is fixed. Re-balance.`,
                    warn_sim_days:          `You chose to run the custom simulation — it needs at least 2 contact days. Add them or skip the simulation.`,
                    warn_engage_range:      `Engagement design must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_coord_negative:    `Coordination investment cannot be negative.`,
                    warn_conviction_range:  `Conviction must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,

                    coach_default:          `Design decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_contract:      `<strong>That is backward design done right.</strong> Contracting a small set of prioritized, measurable objectives — and an explicit out-of-scope — before designing a single module is exactly the discipline this brief needs. Now every module has something to earn its place against.`,
                    coach_r1_everything:    `<strong>You are designing to the vague brief.</strong> Leadership AND market design AND P&L AND change management in 12 days? The CHRO will like the soft parts, the COO will call it not commercial enough — you satisfy no one because you never made them choose. What are the four objectives this program will actually deliver, and what did you tell Pohjola it will <em>not</em> do?`,
                    coach_r1_topics:        `<strong>Designing forward from interesting topics produces incoherent programs.</strong> Faculty availability is not a learning objective. Start from the manager capabilities Pohjola needs, then let those decide the topics — not the other way round.`,
                    coach_r1_sponsor:       `<strong>The loudest sponsor is not the design.</strong> Hand the program to the COO and the CHRO walks; the brief explicitly needs both. Reconcile them into one ranked objective set rather than picking a winner.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp scoping.</strong> A contracted, ranked objective set that both the CHRO and the COO sign — leadership behaviours applied to commercial transition decisions — is the single thing that lets the rest of the design cohere. This is the contract the whole program traces back to.`,
                    coach_r1_tilt:          `<strong>Tilting to one sponsor loses the other.</strong> Pohjola’s brief deliberately holds leadership and commercial together — the transition needs managers who can lead change <em>and</em> make commercial calls. Rank, don’t pick a side.`,
                    coach_r1_both:          `<strong>“Promise both fully” is how the 12 days overflow.</strong> Saying yes to every objective without ranking them just defers the conflict to Round 2, where the days run out. Rank them now, openly, with the sponsors in the room.`,
                    coach_r1_noscope:       `<strong>No out-of-scope list is no design.</strong> If you never say what the program will not do, the modular instinct fills the 12 days with everything. Saying no skilfully is half of custom exec-ed.`,
                    coach_r2_balanced:      `<strong>A backward-designed mix.</strong> Leadership and commercial modules tied to the objectives, a capstone that forces application, and peer action-learning that sustains it — every module traces to a Round 1 objective and the days stay within 12.`,
                    coach_r2_disciplined:   `<strong>Disciplined and deliverable.</strong> A traceable module mix, the simulation where it anchors the commercial objective, and coaching aimed only where it earns its cost — this is richness bounded by the per-learner economics, not maximized for its own sake.`,
                    coach_r2_lecture:       `<strong>This is a lecture-heavy design.</strong> Without a capstone and peer action-learning, senior managers attend but do not apply — and transfer to a live project never happens. Which days force them to <em>do</em>, not just listen?`,
                    coach_r2_skip:          `<strong>Skipping the simulation saves cash but weakens the commercial anchor.</strong> A case study is cheaper but the COO wanted hard market-design capability — make sure something else carries that objective convincingly.`,
                    coach_r2_gold:          `<strong>Two custom simulations is gold-plating.</strong> €130K of simulation to impress the COO eats the budget that should fund the capstone and coaching where they matter. One simulation, tied to the objective, is the disciplined call.`,
                    coach_r2_coachall:      `<strong>You have given all 60 managers 1:1 coaching.</strong> At €1,800 each that is €108K — stacked on the simulation and faculty it pushes past the €669K deliverable budget and your 24% margin evaporates. Which high-touch element earns its cost against an objective, and which is there because it is impressive?`,
                    coach_r2_nocoach:       `<strong>No coaching at all leans hard on peer learning.</strong> That can work and protects the margin — but make sure the managers with the biggest transition roles still get enough individual challenge to drive transfer.`,
                    coach_r3_blend:         `<strong>That is assessment designed for transfer.</strong> A live capstone judged by Pohjola executives proves applied capability to the COO, and the 360° gives the CHRO behavioural evidence — both sponsors get the proof they need, anchored in a real transition project.`,
                    coach_r3_portfolio:     `<strong>Reflective portfolios are cheap but weak proof.</strong> The COO will not accept “managers wrote about what they learned” as evidence of commercial capability. Portfolios alone do not demonstrate transfer to a live project.`,
                    coach_r3_simscore:      `<strong>A simulation score is objective but narrow.</strong> It measures decisions inside the game, not whether capability transferred to Pohjola’s real transition. Pair applied evidence with behavioural evidence for both sponsors.`,
                    coach_r3_exam:          `<strong>A written exam to senior managers is an engagement killer.</strong> It is rigorous and cheap, but executives resist being graded and it proves recall, not transfer — you risk breaching the 90% completion floor and still have no evidence the capability landed on the job.`,
                    coach_r3_no360:         `<strong>Without the 360 the CHRO has no behavioural evidence.</strong> The capstone proves applied capability to the COO, but the CHRO asked for leadership behaviours — €240/participant is a small price for the proof that keeps that sponsor signed.`,
                    coach_r3_lowengage:     `<strong>Rigour without engagement breaches the completion floor.</strong> Twelve days of demands on busy senior managers with no applied, manager-involving design and attendance slides below 90% by the back half. Engineer engagement, not just assessment burden.`,
                    coach_r3_nocoord:       `<strong>A capstone with no coordination is a capstone in name only.</strong> Executive judges, project sponsors and evidence collection do not organize themselves. Credible transfer is coordination-heavy — underfund it and the proof falls apart.`,
                    coach_r4_tradeoff:      `<strong>That is disciplined scope negotiation.</strong> Meeting the COO’s “add two modules” with an explicit trade-off — what comes out, or what the additions cost in days and euros — protects the design, the dates and the margin while still treating the client as a partner.`,
                    coach_r4_cave:          `<strong>You said yes to everything.</strong> Two more modules at the same price and dates means 14 days of content in a 12-day program at a fee that no longer covers it — the schedule is impossible and the margin is gone. What did you offer to take out, or what did you tell the COO the additions would cost?`,
                    coach_r4_refuse:        `<strong>A flat “the design is locked” loses the partnership.</strong> The discipline is not refusing to move — it is making the cost of moving explicit. Offer the trade-off, do not just close the door on a strategic client.`,
                    coach_r4_strong:        `<strong>A steering-group-ready close.</strong> One coherent case — objectives, traced modules, transfer evidence, economics — plus an explicit trade-off on the COO’s demand. That is a design that earns the sign-off and the renewal.`,
                    coach_r4_richness:      `<strong>Leading with richness undercuts the discipline.</strong> Listing everything you packed in invites the COO to add more. Lead with the coherent logic — why these objectives, these modules, this assessment, these economics.`,
                    coach_r4_price:         `<strong>Leading with price reduces the program to a transaction.</strong> Pohjola is buying transition-ready leaders, not module-hours. Anchor the pitch in the capability and the evidence, then let the economics confirm it is sound.`,
                    coach_r4_nokpi:         `<strong>Testimonials are not KPIs.</strong> A strategic renewal turns on hard numbers — objective fit, completion, margin, cost per learner. Commit to measuring them or the steering group cannot defend the spend internally.`,
                    coach_overscoped:       `<strong>Your beautiful curriculum exceeds 12 contact days.</strong> The managers have 12, contractually, and the dates are fixed by Pohjola’s strategy cycle. Part of your program simply cannot be delivered — which modules move to pre-work or digital, and which days are so essential they must be in the room?`,
                    coach_marginbroken:     `<strong>You have spent past the €669K deliverable budget.</strong> The 24% margin is a hard floor — €211K Aurora must hold. Every high-touch element has a cost-per-learner consequence; coherent design optimizes impact subject to the fee, not maximizes richness.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Contracted ranked, measurable objectives with an out-of-scope.`,
                    sum_r1_weak:            `Designed to the vague brief; objectives left unranked.`,
                    sum_r2_good:            `Backward-designed mix, traceable, within 12 days and budget.`,
