                    sum_r3_lecture:         `Exam-led — recall not transfer; completion at risk.`,
                    sum_r3_weak:            `Narrow or weak proof for one of the sponsors.`,
                    sum_r4_good:            `Defended the scope with an explicit trade-off.`,
                    sum_r4_weak:            `Caved to scope creep or lost the partnership.`,

                    insight_coherent:       `Coherent and deliverable: you turned a vague brief into a ranked objective set, designed every module backward from it, proved transfer to a live project, and held the design inside 12 days, the €669K budget and the 24% margin. That is custom exec-ed done as disciplined subtraction.`,
                    insight_undeliverable:  `Impressive but undeliverable: the design quality is high, but you broke a hard constraint — over the 12 days, over budget/margin, or under the 90% completion floor. A program that cannot be delivered inside the envelope is not a program Pohjola can sign.`,
                    insight_incoherent:     `Incoherent program: without a contracted, ranked objective set and modules that trace to it, the design tried to do too much and satisfied neither sponsor. Backward design from prioritized objectives is what makes the rest cohere.`,
                    insight_overscoped:     `You over-scoped the 12 contact days. Contact time is the scarcest resource in executive program design; an over-scoped curriculum simply cannot be delivered, and part of your program would have to be cut after sign-off.`,
                    insight_margin:         `You breached the 24% margin floor. Each design element — simulation, coaching, assessment — has a cost-per-learner consequence; coherent design optimizes impact subject to a fixed fee, not maximizes richness.`,
                    insight_completion:     `Projected completion fell below the contractual 90% floor. A lecture-heavy, assessment-heavy design loses busy senior managers; engagement and applied design are how you keep them to the end.`,
                    insight_transfer:       `Transfer strength stayed low — the program risks producing attendance, not capability. Live application, action learning and manager involvement are what make capability transfer to the job and earn renewal.`,
                    insight_cpl:            `Cost per learner exceeded the ~€11,150 target. High-touch elements stacked indiscriminately blow the per-learner economics; reserve them for where they earn their cost against an objective.`,
                    insight_renewal:        `This is a renewal-worthy design: both sponsors satisfied, transfer proven, and every constraint held. The strategic client — and €880K of pipeline — stays with Aurora.`,

                    hint_overscoped:        `You are over the 12 contact days — the cap is contractual and the dates are fixed. Move content to pre-work or digital and keep only the days that must be in the room.`,
                    hint_margin:            `You are past the €669K deliverable budget and the 24% margin is breaking. Pull back a high-touch element that is not earning its cost against an objective.`,
                    hint_completion:        `Projected completion is below the 90% floor. Lift engagement — applied, manager-involving design — rather than piling on more rigour.`,
                    hint_transfer:          `Transfer strength is weak. Anchor the design in a live capstone and peer action-learning so managers apply, not just attend.`,
                    hint_objfit:            `Objective fit is low — modules are not tracing to ranked objectives. Go back to the learning-objective contract and cut what does not earn its place.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — fit, transfer and sponsor support all healthy. Now hold the line on scope and report the full economics to close the sign-off.`,

